#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a4550c-7ed7-58aa-8928-23dd1cfd0cd7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Monomial"}] :question/id #uuid "368cbbae-4eff-5eff-a8f3-d3e8359447a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-364" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064168 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "818f4fbe-e251-57bd-90ae-b6517c6f2f89" :question/math-academy-id "e-367" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of  $\\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3174cae9-484e-5ca3-8e5c-3c9cddc86145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c25e6037-3900-5314-b9a9-262fef07061c" :question/math-academy-id "e-368" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if $F'(x) = f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}= x^{-3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a4550c-7ed7-58aa-8928-23dd1cfd0cd7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Monomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} {:answer/id #uuid "62de7813-7886-5bed-86cd-1ca23e600b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21{x}^{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513799} {:answer/id #uuid "65428549-ccd7-5201-8026-265c549f0352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value 